WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.900
The world's two largest economies are testing whether their presidents should meet on artificial intelligence or ai, safety, even while they keep racing each other on chips, talent, and military uses of the same technology. This is Omni View, episode 168. No day has been written on a calendar, and no one has said whether this would be a summit, a call, or something quieter in between. Nikkeh Asia treats an early conversation as a practical way

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:48.340
for those two economies to handle shared technical danger without first ending their wider contest. Research labs and ministries in both countries have poured money into models, hardware, and skilled staff, and each capital is under pressure at home to show it can limit harm from a fast rollout.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:57.180
That heavy spending is the ground almost everyone in the reporting accepts. The split is over what any joint rulebook should actually protect.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:10.540
Commentary tied to the Chinese state has, in earlier statements, pushed for global standards that still let poorer countries reach the same tools, rather than locking the best systems inside a handful of wealthy markets.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:23.660
Put in its most serious form, that access argument says safety language can become a polite cover for export bans, and countries still building their industries would then be frozen out of a general purpose technology.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:33.500
People who make that argument still live with a limit they rarely advertise, a standard with no verification can be ignored the moment it pinches a national champion.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:44.760
American officials put the stress elsewhere, locking down power grids, hospitals, and other critical systems, and stopping an adversary from turning the same software into a weapon.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:55.040
In that security framing, you cannot draft joint safety language with arrival, and then pretend chips, cloud, and military code are ordinary trade goods.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:06.640
Officials who argue this way carry a limit of their own. If every control is treated as existential, there is little room left for the narrow technical exchanges that actually stop accidents.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:20.680
The idea, as reported, would stay on safety and risk even while the bigger technology fight continues. Until a date, a format, or a written agenda appears, this remains a trial balloon, not a sign negotiation.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:28.020
Israeli aircraft struck a village in southern Lebanon, and Lebanese state media say 11 people were killed.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:37.680
The raids came after a United States brokered halt in fighting that was supposed to quiet the border between Israel and Hisbalah, the armed movement based in Lebanon.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:45.520
President Joseph Aoun has asked Washington to step back in, saying the truth needs that pressure if it is going to hold.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:53.780
BBC World News treats the raid as a direct test of that ceasefire framework, and records that civilians were among the dead.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:03:02.700
Al Jazeera puts the emphasis on children among those killed, and describes the day as part of a return to fighting rather than a one-off incident.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:11.600
CNBC notes that raids on Sunday alone killed at least seven people, and that those deaths are what pushed the Lebanese president to appeal.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:19.280
Lebanese officials say the attacks cut against the agreement's basic point, which was to bring down violence across the border.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:25.380
Israeli security sources have called the operations answers to specific threats near that border.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.140
The deaths themselves are not in dispute across those accounts.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.140
What remains contested is whether the true language allows this kind of targeted raid.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:42.820
What happens next sits with the ceasefire sponsors. Aoun has asked the United States to re-engage before the agreement erodes further.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:51.120
Ukrainian authorities logged more air raid warnings in a single day than on any other day since the full-scale invasion started.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:59.940
Sirens interrupted schools, shops, and public transport in several regions, as Russian forces kept up missile and drone attacks through the hours.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.860
Everything in the argument turns on what those weapons were aimed at.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:08.480
The BBC dwells on the where this tempo puts on ordinary city life.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:13.240
Closed classrooms, stalled buses, nights spent moving in and out of shelters.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:20.940
Keeves government calls the pattern an effort to grind down public stamina rather than a set of one-off military shots.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:26.560
Russian state media on similar operations have said the targets were military assets.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:33.420
Independent monitoring groups have recorded damage to apartment blocks as well as to sites described as military.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:40.340
The elevated siren count is the shared fact. Intent and proportion are where those named voices part.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:46.700
Shelter systems and local authorities kept running the alert network through the day as the strikes continued.

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:53.540
German factories produced less last month with official figures showing a fall of 1.1%.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:05:04.700
The reading followed a flat month and desk status, the Federal Statistics Agency, said the slide was driven largely by weaker work in car plants, with the numbers released on Monday.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:12.420
The Wall Street Journal ties the contraction to stubborn supply chain trouble and softer orders from important export markets.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:18.040
German business groups have instead pointed at high energy prices and the weight of regulation.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:25.840
Trade unions have asked the government for focused help to protect jobs in manufacturing regions that still live off those plants.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:30.000
Specialists who follow the data keep returning to a structural point.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:38.080
The auto industry remains the spine of the country's export model, so a bad month in that sector shows up quickly in the national total.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:43.260
The next print will show whether this was a one-month dip or the start of a longer slide.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:55.860
Industry forecasts say Japan's AI data center capacity is on course to grow fourfold within seven years, a build out priced at about $60 billion for halls and the power to run them.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:07.840
Large technology firms and utilities are lining up the projects to match expected demand, Nikkei Asia reports, and they are trying to coordinate grid upgrades so the new halls can actually switch on.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:14.040
Government energy planners have named a steady electricity supply as the main break on that timetable.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:18.500
Neighbors near proposed sites have asked about land use and noise.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:22.360
The forecast is built from company's current spending plans.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.540
Cranes will depend on permits and on whether the grid can grow in time.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:33.940
Toyota Motor says it wants profit from work outside car making to rise 40% by the end of fiscal 2030.

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:38.780
The company intends to grow in mobility services and advance materials.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.180
Businesses that today are a smaller slice of its earnings.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:49.080
Nikkei Asia presents the goal as a way to lean less on traditional vehicle sales as global demand shifts.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:54.900
Company leaders have named software and connected services as the places they expect that growth.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:59.720
Some analysts doubt the calendar leaves enough time to scale those new lines,

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.000
and labor representatives have said jobs need to be kept through the change.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:06.380
The target lives in planning papers.

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.200
Later earnings reports will show whether it was met.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:18.960
A weekend vote at the United Nations General Assembly is progress worth knowing for anyone who has ever looked at a classroom globe and assumed the sizes were true.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:25.640
Delegates back to resolution that encourages equal area world maps, including the equal earth projection,

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:30.260
which tries to show land masses at something closer to their real relative area.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:37.900
The aim is to shrink a long visual habit of making places near the equator, including Africa, look smaller than they are.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:41.100
Several member states put their names on the text.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:48.180
All Africa reports that the vote takes aim at distortions that have sat for years in school materials and official documents.

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:52.860
Supporters say honest sizing helps debates on development and climate,

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:56.700
because policy is harder when the map itself misleads the eye.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:02.240
Some map makers note that no single projection can keep every spatial property at once.

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:06.720
Area, shape, and distance cannot all be perfect on a flat page.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:09.520
The resolution has no enforcement clause.

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:13.120
Governments and publishers would adopt it only if they choose to.

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:18.260
To really understand how rival governments start talking about machine safety,

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:23.480
ignore the summit photograph and look at the small technical rooms that usually come first.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:29.720
A lot of coverage treats the choice as all or nothing, a grand bargain or a total freeze.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:37.440
In practice, expert groups often open on narrow chores such as how to test a model and how to report an incident,

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:40.360
long before any president walks into a room.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:45.140
Those tables typically seat government researchers, standards agencies,

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:49.980
and a few companies, not the foreign ministry names you see at a signing ceremony.

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:53.920
Nuclear arms control history is useful calibration here.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:58.080
Early protocols sometimes kept working through ugly political weather,

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:02.560
when both capitals saw a mutual interest in not stumbling into an accident.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:07.280
Early climate talks and aviation safety talks followed a similar pattern,

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:11.880
with a shared technical vocabulary creating a thin but durable channel.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:15.400
So when a headline says, talks on guardrails,

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:20.460
the useful distinction is whether that means formal bargaining, expert level exchanges,

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.900
or merely people floating the idea.

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:26.480
Models and agents covers the AI side daily.

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:32.460
In the next round of stories, look for a named working group with a written technical product and a deadline,

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:35.000
not a general promise to keep talking.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.960
If you want to go deeper on those possible leaders' conversations,

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:43.260
compare how Nikkei, Asia, and Bloomberg are covering the same facts.

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:48.160
You will hear two different frames on how far an exploratory idea actually goes.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:52.740
Tomorrow, watch for whether either capital names a date, a format,

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:55.400
or a working group with a real technical assignment.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:58.580
That's omniview. The news can feel heavy,

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:02.600
but understanding what's actually happening and what's being done about it

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:04.680
beats doom scrolling every time.

